        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "86acd6d4-d156-5daf-b9a2-9bcca9c15d1a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Arne Berge, Finn Moestue Husebye\n- Places: Hamburg, Germany, Sweden\n- Roles: Norwegian priest, seamen's priest, assistant priest\n- Events: World War II, White Buses operation\n- Organizations: Swedish Red Cross, Danish government\n\nFacts:\n- Arne Berge was born on 29 June 1908 and died on 13 August 1988.\n- Arne Berge was a Norwegian priest.\n- During World War II, Finn Moestue Husebye left Germany after a conflict with Nazi-friendly Norwegians in Germany.\n- During World War II, assistant priest Arne Berge took over after Finn Moestue Husebye as the seamen's priest in Hamburg.\n- During World War II, Arne Berge worked among Scandinavian prisoners in Germany while serving as seamen's priest in Hamburg.\n- In the spring of 1945, the White Buses operation was undertaken by the Swedish Red Cross and the Danish government.\n- The White Buses operation rescued concentration camp inmates from areas under Nazi control and transported them to neutral Sweden.\n- Arne Berge helped plan and carry out the White Buses operation.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "86c83c30-9d1b-561c-a6d5-c14d3806e4c0",
        "text": "Page: Arne Berge. Sentence: Arne Berge (29 June 1908 – 13 August 1988) was a Norwegian priest.\nPage: Arne Berge. Sentence: He was seamen's priest in Hamburg during World War II, when he also worked among Scandinavian prisoners in Germany, and helped planning and carrying out the White Buses operation.\nPage: White Buses. Sentence: \"White Buses\" was an operation undertaken by the Swedish Red Cross and the Danish government in the spring of 1945 to rescue concentration camp inmates in areas under Nazi control and transport them to Sweden, a neutral country.\nPage: Finn Moestue Husebye. Sentence: He had to leave Germany because of a conflict with Nazi-friendly Norwegians in Germany, and assistant priest Arne Berge took over after him as the seamen's priest in Hamburg.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
